# A Field Guide to Substrate-Invariant Complexity

Could a computer grown from cells and a computer assembled from silicon disagree about which problems are intrinsically difficult? Begin with the central distinction: a simulation must preserve **meaning** and report its **resource overhead**.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The composed-overhead law is the quantitative engine behind every transport result. Explore its executable form below.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the bounded-class transport argument</summary>
If a source program decides a language within budget $b$, semantic preservation makes its translation decide the same language. The cost inequality and monotonicity give $c_N(T(p),x)\le h(c_M(p,x))\le h(b(x))$. Thus the destination budget is $h\circ b$.
</details>

## Exact equivalence mirrors every hierarchy level

When both translations have identity overhead, every language has the same bounded-class membership on both substrates. The whole hierarchy—not just one favored class—is mirrored.

