A machine model supplies programs, acceptance, and natural-valued cost. A bounded class contains languages decided pointwise within a budget. A simulation preserves acceptance and controls translated cost by a monotone overhead. Exact mutual simulations use identity overhead in both directions. A witness class accepts an input when some certificate is accepted within budget. A jump hierarchy combines current-level membership with successor escape.
